# Responsible-AI notes: RAI[lesson] = (title, html). Shown before "Key takeaways".
RAI = {
 1: ("When not to use machine learning",
     "<p>ML learns whatever patterns are in its data — including unfair ones. In 2018 Reuters reported that Amazon scrapped an experimental hiring model that had learned to downgrade CVs containing the word \"women's\", because it was trained on a decade of mostly male hires. Before reaching for ML, ask: would simple, transparent rules do? Is there good, representative data? Can mistakes be noticed and corrected? If a decision must be explained to the person it affects, or errors are unacceptable, ML may be the wrong tool.</p>"),
 2: ("Turning people into vectors",
     "<p>Representing a person as a list of numbers hides choices. A feature such as a postcode can act as a <strong>proxy</strong> for ethnicity or income even if those columns are removed, so a model can discriminate without ever \"seeing\" a protected attribute. Removing a sensitive column is not enough; check what the remaining features correlate with.</p>"),
 3: ("You get what you optimise",
     "<p>Gradient-based learning makes the loss small — nothing else. If the measurable target is only a stand-in for what you care about, the model will optimise the stand-in (Goodhart's law: \"when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\"). Choosing the loss and the target is a value judgement, not just a technical detail.</p>"),
 4: ("Base rates and small groups",
     "<p>The Bayes demo shows that even an accurate test produces mostly false alarms when the condition is rare. The same maths applies to systems that flag rare behaviour such as fraud or crime: most people flagged may be innocent. Estimates for small subgroups are also far noisier than for the majority, so a model can look accurate overall while being unreliable for minorities.</p>"),
 5: ("Bias starts in the data",
     "<p>A model can only be as fair as its data. Obermeyer et al. (<em>Science</em>, 2019) found that a widely used US healthcare algorithm under-referred Black patients because it predicted future healthcare <em>cost</em> as a proxy for health <em>need</em> — and less had historically been spent on Black patients with the same needs. Ask who is missing from the data, how labels were produced, and whether people consented to their data being used. \"Datasheets for Datasets\" (Gebru et al.) is a useful checklist.</p>"),
 6: ("Coefficients are not causes",
     "<p>A regression weight shows an association in this data, holding the other features fixed — not what would happen if you intervened. Leaving out a relevant variable (omitted-variable bias) can flip a coefficient's sign. Never base a policy decision on a coefficient alone without a causal argument or an experiment.</p>"),
 7: ("The cost of training",
     "<p>Training is computation, and computation uses energy. Strubell, Ganesh and McCallum (2019) estimated that training and tuning some large language models emitted as much CO₂ as several cars over their lifetimes. For small models the cost is trivial, but it's worth asking whether a bigger model or another hyperparameter search is really needed. Fixing random seeds also makes results reproducible and auditable.</p>"),
 8: ("Decisions about people need explanations",
     "<p>Logistic regression is common in credit scoring, where regulations in many countries (for example the EU's GDPR and the US Equal Credit Opportunity Act) give people rights to information about automated decisions that affect them. Its weights are relatively easy to explain. Remember too that the decision threshold is a policy choice: moving it changes who is approved, and it may affect different groups differently.</p>"),
 9: ("Evaluate for every group, not just on average",
     "<p>An overall accuracy can hide large differences between groups. Report precision and recall for each relevant subgroup. Fairness has several competing definitions — equal false-positive rates, equal true-positive rates, calibrated scores within each group — and research by Chouldechova (2017) and by Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan (2016) proved that when base rates differ between groups, these criteria cannot all hold at once. Choosing which one matters is a decision for people, not the algorithm.</p>"),
 10: ("Overfitting leaks private data",
     "<p>A model that memorises its training data can reveal it. Shokri et al. (2017) showed <strong>membership inference attacks</strong>: by probing a model, an attacker can often tell whether a specific person's record was in the training set — sensitive if the dataset is, say, patients with a disease. Regularisation and good generalisation reduce this risk; techniques such as differential privacy give formal guarantees.</p>"),
 11: ("A model that stores its data",
     "<p>kNN keeps every training example and uses it at prediction time, so deploying it means deploying the raw data — with all its privacy obligations, including deleting a person's record if they ask. Distances also treat every feature as meaningful: if a sensitive attribute (or a proxy) is among the features, \"similar people\" will be judged alike for the wrong reasons.</p>"),
 12: ("Language models of who is \"normal\"",
     "<p>Text classifiers learn which words go with which label. Sap et al. (2019) found that tweets written in African-American English were far more likely to be labelled offensive by widely used toxicity datasets and the models trained on them. A spam or moderation filter can silence some groups more than others; test it on varied dialects and writing styles before trusting it.</p>"),
 13: ("Transparent, but not automatically fair",
     "<p>A small tree's rules can be read and audited, which is a real advantage for accountability. But readable is not the same as fair: a split on a proxy feature (such as a postcode) can encode discrimination plainly in the rules. Audit the splits, not just the accuracy.</p>"),
 14: ("Accuracy versus explainability",
     "<p>Forests and boosting are often more accurate than a single tree but much harder to explain. Post-hoc tools (feature importance, SHAP values, partial-dependence plots) help, but they describe the model approximately and can be misleading — permutation importance, for example, spreads credit unpredictably between correlated features. For high-stakes decisions, consider whether a slightly less accurate but interpretable model is the responsible choice.</p>"),
 15: ("Scores are not probabilities",
     "<p>An SVM's output is a distance from the boundary, not a probability, and a large margin is not a guarantee of being right. If a decision needs a probability (\"85% chance of default\"), calibrate the scores and check calibration for each group. And for consequential decisions, keep a human able to review and override the model.</p>"),
 16: ("Clusters are not natural categories",
     "<p>k-means will always return <em>k</em> groups, whether or not real groups exist. Giving clusters labels like \"low-value customers\" can become self-fulfilling if they decide who gets offers or attention. Treat clusters as a description of this dataset under these features, and be careful when they line up with sensitive attributes.</p>"),
 17: ("Reducing dimensions does not anonymise",
     "<p>Compressing or transforming data does not make it anonymous. Narayanan and Shmatikov (2008) re-identified users in the \"anonymised\" Netflix Prize dataset by matching a handful of their movie ratings with public reviews. A few dimensions, or a few rare values, can be enough to single someone out; treat derived features of personal data as personal data.</p>"),
 18: ("Fragile and opaque",
     "<p>Neural networks can be fooled by tiny, deliberately chosen changes to their input: Szegedy et al. (2014) and Goodfellow et al. (2015) showed images altered imperceptibly to humans that networks misclassify with high confidence. Their reasoning is also hard to inspect. For safety-critical uses, test robustness, monitor behaviour after deployment, and don't treat confidence as correctness.</p>"),
 19: ("Generative models raise new questions",
     "<p>Models that generate text and images inherit the biases of their training data, can produce fluent but false statements (\"hallucinations\"), and make convincing fake media easy to create. Their training data raises questions of consent and copyright that courts and regulators are still working through. See <a href=\"recent.html\">Recent developments</a> for a dated overview.</p>"),
 20: ("Responsible deployment",
     "<p>A model in production makes decisions at scale, so document it: <strong>model cards</strong> (Mitchell et al., 2019) record what the model is for, how it was evaluated, for which groups, and its known limits. Monitor for drift and for unequal error rates, watch for feedback loops (a model's decisions changing the data it later learns from), and make sure people affected can question a decision and reach a human.</p>"),
}
